title: It is Mayday in Berlin.
new-page: true
tags: top
go-to: two.scene

Berlin. 1929. You are Karl Brand. You are twenty nine years old. You are attending the Mayday rallies of the twenty ninth year. 

You are not a KPD member. Your wife, Erika Jung, is. You have a baby on the way, you’ll name him Leo, or maybe, if it’s a girl, after Erika’s mother, Maria, Maria was a beautiful lady, heart and soul.

You fondly think of that time you visited her, a few years ago, during hyperinflation, you sat by the fireplace, you and Erika were just twenty two back then, watching the money burn, laughing, having tea. 

It was the moment you fondly remember, Erika’s beauty, her intelligence, her diligence, it all was illuminated by that little fire.

The streets are littered with faces, a hundred or so,  with men and women, even children. Despite everything, there is a sense of gaiety amongst the people. You are walking, hand in hand with your wife, you hear a sudden thunderous noise!

You turn, it’s one of your wife’s friends, Hanna, “Erika! Erika!” She shouts, a smile upon her face. Your wife beams, ear to ear, “Hannah!” She shouts wildly, she lets go of your hand, the two embrace one another, you smile, it’s good to see her like this.

Hannah speaks first, “Did you see Vorwärts last night?” Erika shook her head “No, me and Karl were out to supper.” Hannah sighed “Two hundred dead on May First, criminal plans of Communists! What the hell is that!” Erika spat on the pavement “Damn them, really, trying to stop May Day rallies?” 

You interject “There’s been police all over! I saw an armoured car, with a machine gun on it, drive through Wedding.” Hannah grimaced “They aren’t going to use it, the cowards are pretending.”

Erika nodded, so did you, you didn’t really think the Police would actually do it. “Ladies, ladies,” You said, “Let’s celebrate today, not focus on those frighteners.” Hannah laughed “Yes, Yes, that'd be better, wouldn’t it?” 

The smile returned to your lips, you breathed in, the air was of spring and of smoke. There was a faint coal dust, you weren’t sure if it was there or just in your memory. You didn’t care, the linden trees were budding. 

You felt Erika manoeuvre her hand in yours, warm, they were small too as they laced in with yours. You couldn’t help but giggle, Hannah walked, a bounce in her step, Erika turned to you “Karl,” She said, “The baby is settling, doesn’t want to ruin our day.” 

You laughed “It best, or it’ll have me to answer to.” Erika laughed back, she walked, her face was open, joyous. You enjoyed it, you swore to remember that face, this moment, right here, for so long as you lived.

@two.scene.dry
